# DTF tier manifest: maven-files-casing  (#2706 / #2707)
# Profile-set: s3 / single. A real shared object store (MinIO) — the ONLY
# topology on which the Maven flat-key companion-attribution class manifests.
# On a filesystem backend each repo physically owns its key space, so the
# camelCase-vs-snake_case files[] lookup is short-circuited (flat_key_readable
# returns true unconditionally) and the bug cannot be observed. Same MinIO
# topology as tiers/isolation.
#
# The bug (#2706): the 1.6.0 Maven flat-key attribution resolves a row-less
# legacy GAV companion (.pom/.module/-sources.jar) via its parent artifact's
# metadata files[] array. The upload handler WRITES those entries in camelCase
# (`storageKey`), but the resolver originally matched snake_case (`storage_key`),
# so on CLOUD backends the owner's own GET of the companion 404'd. The fix reads
# COALESCE(f->>'storageKey', f->>'storage_key') + backfills.
#
# Oracle (oracle.sh) is the discriminating gate:
#   POSITIVE  — owner GET of a row-less companion attributed ONLY via the
#               parent's camelCase files[] -> 200 (FAILS pre-#2706, PASSES on rc)
#   ISOLATION — a FOREIGN repo GET of the same shared flat key -> 404
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: the oracle drives admin login + several back-to-back
# Maven PUTs; the base default (ON) would throttle them. Matches tiers/isolation.
PROFILES="storage.s3"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
